{
  "title": "Single-character multilingual tokens vs English function tokens",
  "explanation": "The Group feature fires on isolated single-character tokens and punctuation across scripts (e.g., Vietnamese 'ế', Greek letters ν/ι, single-letter initials and dots), reflecting tokenization boundaries that isolate non‑ASCII or single-letter characters. The Baseline feature instead captures common English function tokens and orthographic separators (e.g., 'of', '-' and capital initials like 'W' or 'U'), so the two features cover different token types with low overlap.",
  "tags": ["single-character", "multilingual", "punctuation", "function-words"]
}

{
  "title": "Group-SAE: structural/section tokens vs Baseline-SAE: legal named entities",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature (layer 13) activates on structural and formatting tokens — punctuation and symbols (')', '$') and document-section words like “equation”, “code”, “provision”, “declaration”, “opinion” — i.e., tokens that mark formal structure across domains (math, code, legal). The Baseline-SAE feature (also layer 13) instead focuses on named actors and organizations common in legal text (\"Defender\", \"attorney\", judge/party names like \"Friedman\", \"Jackson\", \"distributor\"). The moderate Jaccard (0.356) reflects some overlap in legal/formal contexts but a clear split between structural markers versus entity/actor recognition.",
  "tags": ["document-structure", "punctuation", "named-entities"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline / paragraph boundary marker",
  "explanation": "Both features highly activate on the newline token, reflecting formatting boundaries in legal text. Group-SAE (feat. 15367) tends to fire on single-line breaks that separate sentences, inline quotes, or citations (e.g., 'Committee \\n', 'to avoid\\n'), suggesting sensitivity to sentence-level transitions. Baseline-SAE (feat. 4834) more often aligns with double newlines and explicit paragraph or section starts (e.g., '\\n\\n       {¶4}'), indicating detection of paragraph boundaries and structural breaks. The moderate Jaccard (0.39) shows overlap in capturing whitespace cues but different granularities of formatting.",
  "tags": ["linebreak", "paragraph-boundary", "legal-formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Period token in numbering and section headings ('.')",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on the '.' token used in section headings, numbered lists, and enumerations (e.g., '3 . Case Two', '2 . 4. Experiment 1'). They appear to mark document-structural punctuation/numbering rather than lexical content; the Jaccard of 0.306 indicates substantial but not complete overlap in the exact contexts each model captures.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "enumeration", "document-structure", "section-headings"]
}

