{
  "title": "Token-level detector for the word \"trust\"",
  "explanation": "Both features reliably activate on the token 'trust'/'Trust' across many contexts (verb: \"trust me\", noun: \"our trust\", adjective forms like \"trustworthy\"). The snippets show capitalization invariance and usage in interpersonal, institutional, and idiomatic settings. The moderate Jaccard (0.314) suggests substantial overlap—these appear to be largely redundant lexical detectors for the lemma 'trust' rather than features encoding distinct syntactic roles or higher-level sentiment.",
  "tags": ["lexical", "polysemy", "redundancy"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features fire on \"security\" but with different senses",
  "explanation": "Although both features top-activate on the token \"security\" (Jaccard 0.320), the Group-SAE examples concentrate on institutional, political, and personnel senses (Security Council, security guard, security assistance, industry association), while the Baseline-SAE covers a broader set including physical/technical security (locks, security systems, trust company) as well as institutional uses. This indicates the Group feature is more semantically specialized, whereas the Baseline captures a wider range of the word's polysemy.",
  "tags": ["polysemy", "institutional-vs-technical", "semantic-specialization"]
}

{
  "title": "Number-base conversion signal vs. LaTeX/formatting mix",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature cleanly captures the semantic concept of numeric base conversions (token 'base' in question prompts like \"What is X (base A) in base B?\"). The Baseline-SAE feature is noisier: it activates on both base-conversion prompts and formatting/LaTeX tokens (backslash and braces), indicating it mixes semantic content with punctuation/markup artifacts. The Jaccard (0.346) reflects moderate overlap (shared 'base' contexts) but divergent, less interpretable behavior in the baseline.",
  "tags": ["number-bases", "formatting-artifacts", "interpretability"]
}

