raw:
{
  "title": "Asterisk/operator detector in algebraic expressions",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the '*' token in math prompts. Group feature 4381 tends to fire on exponentiation and multiplication patterns (e.g., '**' power notation and variable-power terms) within rewrite/expand tasks, while Baseline feature 871 also detects '*' but is more often seen in multiplication contexts involving zeros, negations, or coefficient manipulations. In short: both mark asterisk-based operators, with Group biased toward power notation and Baseline toward simplification/multiplication contexts.",
  "tags": ["operator", "exponentiation", "algebra"]
}

{
  "title": "Numeric/token detector: coordinates and tabular numbers",
  "explanation": "High Jaccard (0.782) indicates these two features largely overlap: both respond to numeric tokens occurring in coordinates, measurement tables, and short numeric fields (e.g., x/y coordinates, accuracy/acc columns, and numeric lists). The Group-SAE feature is slightly broader — it activates on a wider set of numeric tokens (40, 80, 990 in addition to 20/30/70), suggesting sensitivity to a wider numeric range or multi-digit tokens — whereas the Baseline-SAE concentrates more on a smaller set (notably 70, 30, 20) and appears in contexts like hex/ascii byte sequences and repeated measurement columns.",
  "tags": ["numeric","coordinates","tabular-data"]
}

{
  "title": "Pronoun-focused feature capturing 'he' variants and tokenization artifacts",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly overlap (Jaccard 0.752) and appear to detect the third-person masculine pronoun in its surface forms (\"he\", \"He\", \"him\") as well as punctuation- or tokenizer-attached variants (\">He\", repeated \"he he\", and broken forms like \"he ms\"). Contexts show sentence-initial uses, embedded mentions, and informal/repeated tokens, so the features primarily encode pronoun identity plus sensitivity to capitalization and token-boundary artifacts; differences between Group-SAE and Baseline-SAE are minimal and mostly reflect minor ordering or boundary sensitivity.",
  "tags": ["pronouns", "capitalization", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "Hyphen / hyphenation detector",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to hyphen-like punctuation in compounds, broken words, and ranges (e.g., \"patho - mechanics\", \"neuro - glial\", \"1--2 years\"). The Group feature is primarily tied to the ASCII hyphen-minus ('-'), while the Baseline also activates on a typographic hyphen ('‐'), indicating sensitivity to orthographic/typographic variants and tokenization differences around hyphens.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "hyphenation", "typography"]
}

