raw:
{
  "title": "Topical/section-heading technical nouns (scientific & networking)",
  "explanation": "Both features pick out high-level technical nouns that occur as topic words or section/headline tokens in scientific text (e.g., Network, Analysis, Protocol, Background, ratio, content). The high Jaccard (0.70) shows they encode essentially the same topical/category signal at layer 13. The small difference—Group includes 'Access' while Baseline includes 'protein'—suggests a slight bias in Group toward networking/administrative contexts and in Baseline toward biological jargon, but the core pattern is shared.",
  "tags": ["topic-words", "scientific-text", "section-headings"]
}

{
  "title": "Detector of nominalizing suffixes / abstract nouns",
  "explanation": "Both features pick out noun-forming and abstract-word suffixes (e.g. -ness, -ity, -ality, -ency, -plicity) and whole-word instances like \"uniqueness\" and \"complexity.\" The Group and Baseline largely overlap in function, with the Group emphasizing grammatical/technical abstract forms and the Baseline showing slightly broader lexical instances (e.g. \"robustness\", colloquial \"coolness\"). Overall they act as a morphological/semantic detector for nominalization and abstract nouns.",
  "tags": ["morphology", "suffixes", "nominalization", "abstract-nouns"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect the pronoun \"we\" (first‑person plural)",
  "explanation": "Both Group-SAE and Baseline-SAE strongly activate on the token \"We\"/\"we\", consistently surfacing dialogue, editorial, and collective statements. The high overlap (Jaccard 0.63) shows they encode the same first‑person plural pronoun signal; the Group feature appears slightly more concentrated on conversational/collective usages, while the Baseline is marginally broader across narrative contexts.",
  "tags": ["pronoun", "first-person-plural", "dialogue"]
}

{
  "title": "Slash (/) as a separator/punctuation token",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the '/' token and largely overlap (high Jaccard). They capture the slash as a generic separator in diverse contexts: abbreviations like \"n/a\" or Spanish \"s/n\", code comment terminators like \"*/\", file paths, chemical/element ratios (e.g. \"Sr/Fe\"), and mixed-format tokens created by tokenization. The Group-SAE examples emphasize slashes in short phrases and address/price snippets, while the Baseline-SAE examples show additional coverage of bracketed ratios, multilingual fragments, and end-of-text artifacts, indicating subtle dataset-driven differences but the same core role.",
  "tags": ["punctuation","separator","tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "Adjectival quality / sentiment detector (positive vs negative descriptors)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on short adjectives and quality descriptors (e.g., \"terrible\", \"good\", \"reliable\", \"safe\") and on the morphological suffix \"ious\" (from words like \"obnoxious\"). The Jaccard of 0.637 reflects this substantial overlap. The Group feature is slightly more concentrated on standalone adjectives and articles/determiners (\"the\", \"a\"), while the Baseline feature preserves that core but also includes possessive/context tokens like \"my\" and a longer adjective \"collected\", suggesting the baseline is a bit broader in contextual usages.",
  "tags": ["sentiment", "adjectives", "morphology", "function-words"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / sequence boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features overwhelmingly activate on the <|endoftext|> token across varied contexts (dialogue endings, math/LaTeX tails, citations, code/comments), indicating they detect sequence termination or boundary/formatting markers rather than any specific semantic content. The very high Jaccard (0.988) shows the two units are effectively redundant.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text","sequence-boundary","formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Wiki category token + bracketed markup (Group is broader)",
  "explanation": "Both features detect the 'Category' token common to Wikipedia category lines (hence the high Jaccard). The Group-SAE feature also strongly activates on punctuation-bracket sequences like '.[]{' and '[]{', which occur in Markdown/Pandoc figure or label annotations (image/figure references and data-labels). Thus the Group feature encodes a broader structural/markup signal (categories plus bracketed figure/label syntax), while the Baseline feature is a narrower detector mostly centered on plain 'Category' lines.",
  "tags": ["wiki-category", "markup", "bracketed-punctuation"]
}

{
  "title": "LaTeX brace/matrix punctuation vs Wikipedia 'Category' metadata",
  "explanation": "Group-SAE feature 8171 is picking up punctuation-heavy LaTeX formatting (brace pairs and array/matrix delimiters like '}{', '\\\\begin{array}{...}', '\\\\end{array}') — a syntactic/formatting cue. Baseline-SAE feature 15037 activates on the word 'Category' in wiki pages, a semantic/metadata footer token. Despite a high Jaccard, the former is about punctuation/markup structure while the latter signals document metadata.",
  "tags": ["LaTeX","punctuation","wiki","metadata"]
}

{
  "title": "Sentence-final period detector (near-identical)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the period token at sentence endings across diverse contexts (news, technical, legal, historical). The high Jaccard (0.890) indicates they are effectively the same: detectors for terminal punctuation/sentence boundaries rather than semantic content.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "sentence-boundary", "period"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / sequence-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features (layer 13) overwhelmingly activate on the <|endoftext|> token across diverse snippets (sentence/document endings, code/comment boundaries), suggesting they encode the EOS/sequence-boundary signal rather than semantic content. The very high Jaccard (0.981) indicates the Group and Baseline features learned essentially the same boundary detector.",
  "tags": ["eos","sequence-boundary","surface-token"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / document-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly and almost identically activate on the special end-of-text token (`<|endoftext|>`), across fiction, math, code, tables and lists. With a Jaccard of 0.988 the Group and Baseline units appear to implement the same signal: a generic document/segment terminator detector that fires at the model's end-of-document token regardless of the preceding content.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "document-boundary", "terminator"]
}

{
  "title": "Document-boundary detector: <|endoftext|>",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the special end-of-text token across many contexts, indicating they detect sequence/document boundaries. The near-perfect overlap (Jaccard 0.978) and identical top token suggest these are effectively duplicate layer-13 detectors for the same control token.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text","boundary-token","near-duplicate"]
}

{
  "title": "Colon token detecting QA/formatting separators",
  "explanation": "Both features (layer 13) strongly activate on the colon token (\":\") and primarily pick up instances where colons are used as formatting/metadata markers—notably the \"Q **:**\" question separators seen in scraped QA posts—as well as colons in code snippets and headings. The high Jaccard (0.865) indicates the Group and Baseline features are nearly identical in behavior, capturing syntactic/formatting punctuation rather than semantic content.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "QA-separator", "formatting"]
}

