raw:
{
  "title": "Group feature isolates the subword \"an\"; Baseline mixes casing and longer substrings",
  "explanation": "With Jaccard 0.304, the Group-SAE feature is a compact, interpretable detector for the subword \"an\" (both lowercase and uppercase) appearing as an affix or interior substring across languages and contexts. The Baseline feature covers the same core but also activates on longer fragments like \"Lan\" and \"anan\" and on varied capitalizations, indicating a more entangled, less focused representation that conflates the core \"an\" subtoken with surrounding characters and named-entity/fragments.",
  "tags": ["subword", "casing", "morphology", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect the common token \"home\" across senses",
  "explanation": "Both Group-SAE (layer 13) and Baseline-SAE (layer 14) strongly activate on the token variants \"home\"/\"Home\" in many surface contexts (titles, compounds, possessions, e.g. \"Home Again\", \"home team\", \"home movies\", \"home owner's exemption\"). The Jaccard of ~0.4 shows substantial overlap in top examples, suggesting they latch onto the frequent, polysemous word rather than a single narrow syntactic role; the layer difference may reflect slight shifts in contextual sensitivity (Group-SAE appearing to emphasize multiword/compound usages while Baseline is a broader distributional detector).",
  "tags": ["common-word", "polysemy", "capitalization", "compound-usage"]
}

{
  "title": "Period/dot detector (URLs, domains, emails)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token '.' and mark punctuation used as a delimiter in web and technical text. Top snippets show periods inside URLs, domain names, email addresses, file paths and bibliographic/metadata lines. The Jaccard 0.34 indicates moderate overlap: they capture the same period-as-boundary signal but at adjacent layers and with slightly different emphases (Group-SAE examples skew toward web/publishing URLs, Baseline-SAE includes more code/comments and academic/site listings).",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "urls/domains", "email"]
}

{
  "title": "Discourse/connective tokens (Group) vs. complementizer \"that\" (Baseline)",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature (layer 13) activates on sentence-initial discourse markers and connectors—tokens like \"Anyway\", \"event\", \"fact\" and punctuation—capturing informal topic shifts, hedges, and discourse-level transitions. The Baseline-SAE feature (layer 14) mainly flags the complementizer/marker \"that\", a closed-class syntactic token used to introduce clauses. The low Jaccard (0.202) reflects that the group feature encodes discourse-level pragmatic signals while the baseline feature encodes a frequent grammatical connector.",
  "tags": ["discourse-markers", "complementizer", "punctuation", "syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Structural/punctuation & code-like tokens vs. colloquial/UI tokens",
  "explanation": "Feature 1066 (Group-SAE) responds to punctuation and structure-focused tokens — carets, dashes, scope resolution (::), short letter/number tokens and table/code contexts (superscripts, numeric columns, code snippets). Feature 6353 (Baseline-SAE) instead lights up on lexical fragments and UI/colloquial content — profanity or emotive words, UI class/filepath pieces, underscores and colons from markup or chat. The only modest overlap is the token \"os\", explaining the low Jaccard (0.266); otherwise the group captures formatting/code structure while the baseline captures surface-level words and UI/vernacular fragments.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "code", "profanity", "markup"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline / section-break detector (Group vs Baseline)",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on newline tokens that mark paragraph or section boundaries (e.g. double newlines, ' **\\n** ' separators, ad/trending blocks and social-embed breaks). The Group feature (layer 13) seems biased toward stronger structural/formatting separators (ads, embeds, bold divider lines), while the Baseline feature (layer 14) more often flags ordinary paragraph or sentence breaks and quote/line endings. The low–moderate Jaccard indicates overlap in boundary detection but different contextual specificity.",
  "tags": ["newline", "section-break", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Bracketed numeric citation detector (Group vs Baseline)",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to bracketed numeric citation tokens (e.g. [1], [2], [3]) commonly found in legal texts and footnote-style references. The Group-SAE (layer 13) is slightly broader — it includes a higher-ranked token ('18') and picks up more varied citation-like patterns (page ranges, exhibit/patent refs, and multi-digit markers), whereas the Baseline-SAE (layer 14) focuses more narrowly on the common single-digit bracketed references. The moderate Jaccard (0.304) reflects strong thematic overlap (citation markers) but differences in token coverage and contextual breadth.",
  "tags": ["citation-markers", "legal-text", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "'.' token — URL/domain and sentence-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the '.' token occurring in URLs, domain names, file paths, RSS feeds and sentence/file punctuation. The moderate Jaccard (0.342) indicates they capture similar punctuation contexts but not identically: Group (layer 13) seems tuned toward dots inside web addresses and code-like links, while Baseline (layer 14) is a nearby dot detector with broader sentence/file-boundary sensitivity. Both likely signal delimiters useful for tokenization and boundary cues.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "URL", "boundary", "domain"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline / Paragraph-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the newline token, marking paragraph breaks, blank lines, and formatting boundaries (e.g., separated sentences, code fences, list/quote breaks). They appear to encode structural whitespace rather than semantic content.",
  "tags": ["newline","formatting","whitespace","structure"]
}

{
  "title": "Period token ('.') — punctuation in code/import/member-access contexts",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the period token '.' appearing in code-like snippets (imports, package declarations, member access). The moderate Jaccard (0.414) reflects substantial overlap in function. Group feature 7098 (layer 13) appears to capture a more general punctuation/delimiter signal across languages and docs (including library docs and web-like contexts), while Baseline feature 1031 (layer 14) looks similar but is slightly skewed toward Java/GUI/Android import patterns.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "code", "imports", "dot_operator"]
}

