{
  "title": "Activations on single-letter variable/function tokens in symbolic math",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to single-letter tokens used as variable or function names in short symbolic-math prompts (e.g., \"What is m(c)?\", nested calls, simple polynomial evaluations). The Group feature (14896, layer 13) has a broader spread of letters (including less common tokens like v, b, j, x) and highlights nested-call/derivative contexts, while the Baseline feature (9915, layer 14) concentrates on a smaller set of common variable tokens (u, o, w, i, m, l, q) and straightforward evaluation examples. A Jaccard of 0.481 shows moderate overlap in the core signal (shared variable tokens) with complementary coverage of rarer letters and contexts.",
  "tags": ["single-letter-variables", "symbolic-math", "function-calls"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect the surface token “Start/start” across UI, command, and code contexts",
  "explanation": "Both Group-SAE and Baseline-SAE primarily activate on the orthographic token 'Start'/'start' in a variety of surface contexts (UI labels, registry keys, command names, function/variable names, and short CLI verbs). The Jaccard 0.55 shows substantial overlap: they behave like token-identity detectors rather than encoding a distinct semantic sense, producing largely redundant features that respond to capitalization and token-boundary occurrences across UI/command/programming snippets.",
  "tags": ["token-identity", "UI/command", "redundant", "orthography"]
}

{
  "title": "Comma usage: clause-boundary and discourse-marker punctuation",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on the comma token and generally mark clause boundaries and parenthetical separators. The Group-SAE feature (layer 13) appears concentrated on commas that introduce or isolate discourse/connective adverbs and contrastive phrases (e.g., before 'however', 'therefore', 'especially') and on commas around citations/parentheticals. The Baseline feature (layer 14) also picks up those cases but is broader, covering commas in technical enumerations, inline citations, and transitional phrases like 'on the other hand'. The Jaccard of 0.554 reflects this moderate overlap with distinct emphases.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "discourse", "clause-boundary", "syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Equals-plus-quote pattern (=\" ) in markup/attributes",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token sequence =\" (equals followed by a quote) that appears in markup-like fragments (e.g., ref-type=\"ref\", width=\"0.999\\linewidth\", file attributes and citation/footnote tags). This reflects sensitivity to formatting/HTML/LaTeX attribute syntax and not to semantic content; the moderate Jaccard (0.473) shows overlap in documents but different layer-specific encodings.",
  "tags": ["markup","punctuation","references","formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Short-character / subword fragment detector (suffixes, small function tokens)",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to very short tokens and subword fragments (e.g., 'at', 'it', 'I') that occur as stand-alone words, roman numerals/capital I, or inside longer words (suffixes like '-at' in 'atized' or 'definiteness'). The group feature additionally surfaces a diacritic token ('á'), suggesting slightly stronger sensitivity to multilingual character forms, while the baseline includes 'is' and lowercase 'i'. Overall they act as low-level character/subword detectors rather than capturing coherent lexical semantics.",
  "tags": ["subword","morphology","multilingual"]
}

{
  "title": "Group-SAE: broad lexical/orthographic cluster vs Baseline-SAE: domain-specific topical cluster",
  "explanation": "With Jaccard 0.478 the two features overlap on general concrete nouns (plants, process, tube, plate, dot) but diverge in focus. The Group-SAE feature groups short, cross-domain lexical items and orthographic/morphological fragments (oil, $, ism, angles, galaxy) suggesting a syntactic/lexical or symbol-aware cluster of physical objects and token forms. The Baseline-SAE feature is more topical and domain-specific (cerevisiae, years, government, ellers, punctuation like \")\"), indicating a tendency to pick up named entities, domain content words, and punctuation markers rather than pure morphological fragments.",
  "tags": ["semantic-clustering", "morphological-fragments", "domain-specific"]
}

{
  "title": "Opening bracket '[' — marker for links, citations, and inline metadata",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on the '[' token used to open bracketed constructs in text: Markdown/HTML links, figure/table/citation refs, code/list fragments, footnotes, and inline annotations. The Group feature (layer 13) and Baseline (layer 14) are highly overlapping (Jaccard 0.644); the Group variant leans slightly toward formal/doc-style uses (figures, exceptions, citations), while the Baseline covers a broader mix including informal/forum attributions and single-letter bracketed items.",
  "tags": ["brackets", "markup", "references"]
}

{
  "title": "Lowercase variable token detector (math/algebra)",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on single-letter lowercase tokens used as variables in elementary algebra/math problems — letters appearing in arithmetic equations, multiplications, digit questions, and variable assignments (e.g., \"6*p - m = 12\", \"3*g = 2*r + 13\"). The Jaccard of 0.657 shows substantial overlap. The Group feature (layer 13) leans toward standalone variable occurrences in solved equations and digit/unit questions, while the Baseline feature (layer 14) appears more often in function-definition or argument positions (e.g., \"g(q)\", \"j(p)\").",
  "tags": ["single-letter variables", "math/algebra", "function-arguments", "equations"]
}

{
  "title": "Subword / single-letter token detector (I, i, ir, Ind, fil, ins)",
  "explanation": "Both features largely detect short orthographic units and subword tokens (single letters like I/i, chemical/abbrev forms like IR/ir, and short prefixes/substrings such as Ind, fil, ins). The high Jaccard (0.756) reflects strong overlap in these surface-form cues; the Group variant leans slightly more on 'Ins/ins' forms while the Baseline shows more instances of the standalone pronoun/identifier 'I' and an extra 'DI' token, but functionally they act as character/subword detectors across abbreviations, acronyms, and word fragments.",
  "tags": ["subword", "orthography", "abbreviation"]
}

{
  "title": "Discourse/temporal determiners (this/that/some/very/a)",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on short determiners and emphasis tokens that occur in temporal or discourse-location phrases (e.g., \"at this/that/some point/time\", \"at very least\"). The group and baseline largely overlap in capturing these discourse markers; the Group feature is slightly broader (includes \"a\"). They appear to mark temporal/location-of-discourse or emphasis boundaries rather than lexical content.",
  "tags": ["discourse-marker", "temporal", "determiner"]
}

{
  "title": "Android/encoding vs. LaTeX label contexts",
  "explanation": "Group feature 5743 activates on Android API identifiers and surrounding punctuation/hyphen patterns (e.g., android.R.*, XML/encoding declarations and 'utf-8' style tokens), indicating a detector for Android/markup namespaces and dash/encoding tokens. Baseline feature 3583 centers on the LaTeX \\label token appearing in math and document referencing contexts, capturing equation and cross‑reference structure. The Jaccard (0.608) reflects moderate overlap in that both pick up metadata/markup tokens, but they specialize on different domains (Android/code/encoding vs. LaTeX/math labels).",
  "tags": ["android","encoding","latex","math"]
}

{
  "title": "Citation/journal-token detector — group feature is PONE‑specialized, baseline is more general",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to inline bibliographic/citation tokens (e.g., [@pone...], figure/table anchors), but Group-SAE feature 8886 is narrowly specialized to the token 'pone' (PLOS ONE references and table/figure anchors), whereas Baseline-SAE feature 2274 activates across multiple journal abbreviations ('pbio', 'pcbi', 'pone'), indicating a more distributed citation/journal detector. The difference suggests group-training encourages a highly focused neuron for a single journal token while the baseline spreads that signal over a feature that covers several journal tags.",
  "tags": ["citation-tokens", "journal-abbreviation", "specialization", "sparsity"]
}

{
  "title": "Colon token — Q/A delimiter (Group) vs general punctuation (Baseline)",
  "explanation": "Both features top-activate on the ':' token (Jaccard 0.634), but they differ in specificity. Group feature 379 (layer 13) concentrates on the forum Q/A delimiter pattern (e.g., \"Q **:**\" and other markup-like occurrences), signalling a feature tuned to question/answer formatting and metadata. Baseline feature 8884 (layer 14) also responds to ':' but across broader contexts (labels, timestamps, code punctuation), behaving like a general colon/punctuation detector. The group feature is therefore more context-specific while the baseline is more distributed and generic.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "Q/A-format", "context-specific"]
}

