{
  "title": "Group feature picks up coherent topical nouns; baseline mixes content with noisy/abbreviations",
  "explanation": "Despite a high Jaccard overlap (0.736), the Group-SAE feature (layer 13) concentrates on concrete, content-bearing nouns spanning domains (e.g., starvation, elderly, competition, diameter, law, cisplatin), yielding a semantically coherent topical signal. The Baseline-SAE feature (layer 14) contains a similar few content tokens (cisplatin, date) but is noisier, including single-letter tokens, abbreviations, and proper‑noun/metadata-like items (s, HC, Peninsula, axis), indicating less focused semantic representation.",
  "tags": ["semantic-coherence", "content-nouns", "abbreviation-noise"]
}

{
  "title": "Colon token — answer/label separator (e.g. \"A:\")",
  "explanation": "Both features primarily activate on the colon token (\":\"), often appearing as an answer or label separator like \"A:\" or in short Markdown/inline formatting. This likely marks discourse/formatting boundaries in posts (answer labels, code snippets, or inline punctuation) rather than semantic content.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "answer-label", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Sentence/end-of-text boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on tokens and contexts that mark the end of a unit of text: the Group feature is dominated by the explicit <|endoftext|> token, while the Baseline feature responds to sentence-final punctuation, closing quotes, newlines and other terminal symbols (., ?\", \\n, $ in end contexts). The very high Jaccard similarity means they are detecting the same signal—textual boundaries and termination/closure cues useful for segmenting or terminating generation.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "sentence-boundary", "punctuation", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Both detect wiki 'Category' lines; Group-SAE is more focused",
  "explanation": "High overlap: both features strongly activate on Wikipedia category lines marked by the token \"Category\". The Group-SAE feature is more narrowly focused on the category label pattern (capitalized \"Category\" with colon/line context), while the Baseline feature is broader—also responding to other common tokens like \"under\" and mixed in license or prose contexts—indicating lower specificity despite similar top activations.",
  "tags": ["wiki-category","formatting","specificity-vs-breadth"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect Wikipedia 'Category:' lines",
  "explanation": "Both Group-SAE (layer 13) and Baseline-SAE (layer 14) strongly activate on the token \"Category\" and surrounding wiki-category snippets (e.g., \"Category:...\" lines and their punctuation/markup). The examples show these units are picking up a structural/formatting cue from Wikipedia pages (capitalization, colon, newline/bold markers), and the high Jaccard (0.815) indicates substantial token overlap and a shared function across the two features.",
  "tags": ["wiki-markup", "structure-marker", "high-similarity"]
}

{
  "title": "Document/sentence boundary detector (end-of-text/punctuation)",
  "explanation": "Both features act as terminal-boundary detectors: the Group-SAE is narrowly tuned to the explicit end-of-text token (<|endoftext|>), while the Baseline-SAE responds to sentence/document-final cues (periods, closing quotes, newlines and other terminal tokens across genres). Together the activations indicate a high-level signal used to mark segment endings or sentence-final contexts for downstream processing.",
  "tags": ["end-of-document","sentence-final","punctuation"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect the in-text 'ref' markup token (high overlap)",
  "explanation": "Both Group-SAE (layer 13) and Baseline-SAE (layer 14) strongly activate on the same inline reference/markup token 'ref' (e.g., {ref -type=\"fig\"}, {ref -type=\"ref\"}, media refs). The high Jaccard (0.858) indicates they represent essentially the same concept; any difference is subtle—baseline examples show a few more media/ref-type variants, while the group feature is similarly focused on figure/citation markup.",
  "tags": ["reference-token", "markup", "figure-citation"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / sentence-final punctuation detector",
  "explanation": "Both features mark sentence or document boundaries. Group 8638 responds almost exclusively to the EOS token (<|endoftext|>), while Baseline 9092 responds to sentence-final punctuation and terminal tokens (., \" , ?, newline) and a few genre-terminal words like “invention” or “$”. The very high Jaccard (0.991) indicates they implement the same boundary-detection role, with the baseline showing extra genre/formatting cues.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "sentence-boundary", "punctuation"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-sequence / sentence-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features reliably mark sequence and sentence boundaries. Group feature 7473 mainly fires on the explicit end-of-text token (<|endoftext|>), while Baseline feature 9092 fires on sentence-final punctuation and boundary markers (., '\"', newlines, question marks, etc.). The very high Jaccard (0.995) indicates they capture essentially the same structural contexts, with the baseline showing a few extra dataset-specific activations (e.g., words like \"invention\" or \"$\") likely due to corpus bias.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "sentence-boundary", "punctuation"]
}

{
  "title": "Location/room detector — Group favors outdoor/structural nouns, Baseline favors indoor/furniture",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to place-related nouns (bed, room, patio) but diverge: Group-SAE (layer 13) activates on broader structural and outdoor-adjacent locations (porch, shack, bank, deck, locker, bathroom), while Baseline-SAE (layer 15) emphasizes indoor room types and furniture/fixtures (desk, couch, bedroom, chamber, window). The moderate Jaccard (0.379) reflects this partial overlap (shared ‘room’/‘bed’/‘patio’) alongside distinct specializations, suggesting Group captures coarser scene/location cues and Baseline refines indoor/object-level context.",
  "tags": ["spatial-nouns", "indoor-vs-outdoor", "room-furniture"]
}

{
  "title": "Group: dash/en‑dash title separators vs Baseline: colon label separators",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature activates on hyphens and en‑dashes (and sometimes colons) used as title or headline separators (e.g., 'Topic - Subtitle' or 'Topic – Subtitle'), capturing stylistic divider tokens in headings and list-like titles. The Baseline-SAE feature mainly activates on colons used as label/metadata separators (e.g., 'Price:', 'Prep:', 'Warnings:'), marking key:value-style fields. The low Jaccard (0.211) reflects that one feature tracks dash-style headline separators while the other tracks colon-based labels/metadata.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "section-separators", "headings", "metadata"]
}

{
  "title": "Organization/institution detector — baseline is cleaner, group adds subword multilingual signals",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to tokens naming organizations (Center, Bureau, Association, Office, Centre, Institute, Agency). The Group-SAE feature additionally activates on subword fragments and French morphemes (e.g., \"ut\", \"ité\", \"que\", \"ariat\"), suggesting sensitivity to word-final pieces and multilingual morphology; the Baseline-SAE is a more focused detector of common English institution nouns.",
  "tags": ["organization-names", "subword-morphology", "multilingual", "entity-detection"]
}

{
  "title": "Prepositional tokens marking possession/agency (legal/formal contexts)",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to high-frequency prepositions ('of', 'by', and for the group feature also 'on') that commonly mark possession, agent, or oblique roles. Group feature 6471 (layer 13) concentrates on short, formulaic sequences like 'on the part of' and other legal/formal phrasings, while Baseline feature 13445 (layer 15) also captures 'by'/'of' but in a broader set of agentive and possessive contexts. The moderate Jaccard (0.297) reflects overlap on common prepositions but divergence in preferred n-gram patterns and register.",
  "tags": ["prepositions", "function-words", "legal-register", "syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Block-closing / else-branch detector (Group vs Baseline)",
  "explanation": "Group feature 9867 centers on the closing-brace '}' token and activates in contexts with else/else-if blocks, indicating a focus on block boundaries or control-flow termination across languages. Baseline feature 14108 also responds to '}', but importantly it includes tab characters as top tokens, so it captures indentation/whitespace patterns alongside block closings—mixing syntactic boundary detection with formatting/style cues. The moderate Jaccard shows overlap around else/} contexts, with the baseline adding whitespace sensitivity.",
  "tags": ["block-boundary", "control-flow", "indentation", "whitespace"]
}

