{
  "title": "Comma: discourse/technical punctuation",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the comma token appearing as a discourse or clause separator in technical/academic text (e.g., after markers like “Finally,” “Next,” “Furthermore,” “Moreover,” and in LaTeX/math-adjacent prose). The Jaccard (0.510) shows moderate overlap: they share the same punctuation focus but differ in distribution — Group feature (layer 15) is more concentrated in LaTeX/math-heavy snippets and formal argument transitions, while the Baseline feature (layer 13) appears across a broader range of scientific prose (papers, patents, astronomy captions).",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "discourse-marker", "math-latex", "technical-prose"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline / formatting separator",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on the newline token used as a document/layout separator (e.g., '**\\n**', blank lines, line breaks). Group-SAE examples emphasize book/publisher metadata and printed-layout breaks, while Baseline-SAE examples show technical docs, command/output blocks, and instructional formatting. They capture the same structural role of newlines across genres.",
  "tags": ["newline", "formatting", "layout", "whitespace"]
}

{
  "title": "Detection of PLOS ONE citation token ('pone') in reference brackets",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token 'pone' appearing inside bracketed citation markers (e.g., [@pone.0102872-World1], [@pone.0048224-Lyra1]). The Group feature (layer 15) concentrates on recurring PLOS ONE reference patterns across many adjacent citations, suggesting a higher-level pattern for bibliographic tokens or citation clusters; the Baseline feature (layer 13) captures the same journal-token but appears slightly broader in context and punctuation handling. The Jaccard of 0.648 indicates substantial overlap (same citation marker) with some divergence in contextual scope and abstraction level.",
  "tags": ["citation","bibliography","formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Single-letter 'k' / 'K' — orthographic surface token",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the literal token 'k' or 'K' across many surface contexts (word-initial fragments like \"kiss\"/\"knew\", verb stems like \"killed\", product names/labels like \"KNIFE\" or \"Ketchup\", code identifiers such as \"KMSKeyName\", and isolated/chat letters). The 0.601 Jaccard score shows substantial overlap: they largely capture the same orthographic signal (case/position and single-letter occurrences) rather than a coherent semantic concept. The Group-SAE examples emphasize terse/morphological fragments and some code uses, while the Baseline-SAE examples include more varied register (recipes, forum signatures, proper names), but the underlying pattern is surface-form sensitivity to the letter token.",
  "tags": ["orthography", "tokenization", "surface-form"]
}

{
  "title": "Numeric/enumeration detector — both capture digits, baseline is broader",
  "explanation": "With Jaccard 0.639 the two features overlap substantially: both activate on short numeric tokens used as list indices, section numbers, timestamps (e.g. “02:18”, “03:05”), dates and small numeric codes. The Group-SAE feature (layer 15) appears tighter — focused on enumerators/timestamps and repeated small tokens — while the Baseline-SAE (layer 13) covers a wider set of numeric contexts (additional digits like 05/07/09, phone numbers, CSS/hex-like strings and table/formatting numbers), suggesting the baseline feature is a more general numeric/format detector and the group feature a more specific enumerator/timestamp detector.",
  "tags": ["numeric-enumeration", "timestamps", "formatting-noise"]
}

{
  "title": "Escaped punctuation / punctuation+backslash in LaTeX/math markup",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on short tokens that are punctuation characters immediately followed by a backslash (e.g. \"}\\\", \"-\\\", \",&\\\", \"^+\\\"). These occur in LaTeX/math/markup contexts (set delimiters, superscripts/subscripts, operator notation and escaped characters) and mark boundaries where punctuation and escape/backslash sequences meet. The Group feature leans toward closing delimiters and punctuation (e.g. \"}\\\", \">\\\"), while the Baseline also includes related tokens like underscores and \")+\\\"; the high Jaccard shows substantial overlap in this escaped-punctuation role.",
  "tags": ["latex", "punctuation", "math", "token-boundary"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline / paragraph-break detector with genre bias",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the newline token (paragraph breaks). The Group-SAE (layer 15) examples skew toward fiction and dialogue or scene transitions, while the Baseline-SAE (layer 13) examples come from news/technical reporting. The high Jaccard (0.714) shows substantial overlap in function (marking paragraph boundaries) with a modest genre-specific specialization.",
  "tags": ["newline", "paragraph-break", "genre-bias", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Verb/action detector — focused agricultural/commercial vs. broad technical verbs",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature (layer 15) is a narrow verb/agent detector centered on agricultural and commercial language (high activation on 'grow'/'growers' and 'sell', with occasional 'govern' in legal/political contexts). The Baseline-SAE feature (layer 13) covers a broader class of action/process verbs found across math/engineering and legal text ('expand', 'transmit', 'reconstruct', 'integrate', 'identify', 'decode', 'embed') while still overlapping on 'grow' and 'govern'. The Jaccard of 0.648 reflects this partial overlap: both detect verbs/actions, but the Group feature is more domain-specific and agent-focused whereas the Baseline is more polysemous and operation-oriented.",
  "tags": ["verbs", "agriculture", "technical", "domain-specific"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline / paragraph-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the newline token and mark paragraph/line breaks (structural formatting) rather than semantic content. The Jaccard of 0.617 shows substantial overlap; the Group feature tends to appear in technical/academic contexts (figures, citations, parentheticals) while the Baseline appears more in general news/political prose, but both signal paragraph transitions, quotes, or editorial breaks.",
  "tags": ["newline", "formatting", "paragraph-boundary", "structural"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / document-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly and selectively activate on the special end-of-text token (<|endoftext|>) across varied contexts (after names, equations, code, citations, lists). The very high Jaccard (0.984) indicates near-identical behavior between the Group-SAE (layer 15) and Baseline-SAE (layer 13): they represent a dedicated neuron for the document/sequence boundary token (a tokenization artifact useful for terminating or delimiting generation).",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "boundary-detection", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text (EOS) / document-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the special token <|endoftext|> across diverse contexts (literary endings, code/math snippets, citations and lists), indicating they encode the document/sentence boundary or EOS marker. The very high Jaccard (0.981) shows Group-SAE and Baseline-SAE learned essentially the same boundary/special-token signal at nearby layers.",
  "tags": ["end-of-document","boundary","special-token","punctuation"]
}

{
  "title": "EOS / sequence-boundary detector (both models)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly and specifically activate on the <|endoftext|> token across genres (narrative, dialog, code, math, tables, bibliographic lines), indicating they act as end-of-sequence/boundary detectors. The extremely high Jaccard (0.976) shows the Group-SAE (layer 15) and Baseline-SAE (layer 13) have effectively learned the same EOS-specialized feature.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text","boundary token","high overlap"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / sequence-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features highly overlap (Jaccard 0.978) and exclusively top-activate on the <|endoftext|> token in diverse contexts (fiction, code, math, tables). This indicates the neuron is detecting the sequence/document termination or hard context boundary rather than any semantic content.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "sequence-boundary", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text (EOS) / document-boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly and consistently activate on the <|endoftext|> token across diverse contexts (narrative endings, code/math, lists, bibliographic fragments). With a Jaccard of 0.97 they are essentially the same signal—a domain-agnostic sequence-termination / document-boundary detector (layers differ but functional role is identical).",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "boundary", "sequence-termination"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature encodes diverse subword/morpheme pieces; baseline captures diacritic vowels",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE (layer 15) activates on a variety of short subword fragments and affix-like sequences (e.g., as, aj, ids, ji, el and single-letter ī/ē), suggesting it represents morphological or lexical pieces. The Baseline-SAE (layer 13) mainly responds to isolated Latvian diacritic vowels (ā, ē, ī), indicating a more character-level/orthographic signal. The higher-layer group feature is therefore more specialized toward meaningful subword units, while the baseline is a more generic character detector.",
  "tags": ["subword", "diacritics", "morphology", "higher-layer"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / sequence-boundary detector (near-duplicate)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the special end-of-text token (<|endoftext|>) and appear in contexts that terminate passages (sentence/document endings, code/list boundaries). The very high Jaccard indicates they are effectively detecting the same sequence-boundary signal despite being in different layers, i.e., a redundant end-of-text/boundary detector.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "boundary-detector", "redundant-feature"]
}

{
  "title": "End-of-text / document boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the <|endoftext|> token across diverse contexts (narrative endings, code snippets, math/citation tails, lists), indicating they encode a document-terminal or boundary marker. The very high Jaccard (0.987) shows Group-SAE and Baseline-SAE learned effectively identical detectors despite being in different layers.",
  "tags": ["end-of-text", "boundary", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature captures discourse/legal contexts; baseline shows noisy/morphological fragments",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature (5660) clusters functional and semantic tokens that occur in discourse or legal/administrative contexts (into, events, hearing, gap, reasoning) and even a Chinese route/token (由), suggesting a coherent high-level concept (document structure, proceedings, or argumentation). The Baseline-SAE feature (7267) is noisier: it contains partial stems, encoding artifacts, and multilingual fragments (, ven, prejud, cern, 生) alongside shared tokens like agreements and 由. The Jaccard overlap (~0.32) reflects a modest shared signal about formal/legal documents but shows the Group feature is more semantically coherent while the Baseline picks up orthographic/encoding and subword noise.",
  "tags": ["discourse", "legal-context", "multilingual", "encoding-noise"]
}

{
  "title": "Both detect “injury” — Group focuses singular contexts; Baseline covers plurals and technical collocations",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token lemma \"injury\" (hence the moderate Jaccard), but they diverge in breadth. The Group-SAE (layer 15) centers on singular, general uses of \"injury\" across sports, legal, and insurance prose (informal/episodic mentions). The Baseline-SAE (layer 14) is broader: it also activates on the plural \"injuries\" and on tighter medical/technical collocations (e.g., \"traumatic injury\", \"chemical injury\", dataset/code contexts), indicating sensitivity to morphological variants and domain-specific phrases.",
  "tags": ["surface-form", "pluralization", "domain-specific"]
}

{
  "title": "Mathematical connective / equation-reference tokens",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on short, high-frequency tokens that occur around equation mentions in mathematical LaTeX/proof text (e.g., “Equation(s)”, “using”, “of”, “to”, “in”). They appear to mark referential language and connective phrasing surrounding displayed or referenced equations. The Group-SAE (layer 15) shows a slight tilt toward prepositions like “of”/“in”, while the Baseline-SAE (layer 14) includes plurals such as “Equations”; the moderate Jaccard overlap (0.375) reflects this shared role with small differences in token preference.",
  "tags": ["math-text", "function-words", "reference-markers"]
}

{
  "title": "Markup- and tokenization-sensitive group feature vs broader technical baseline",
  "explanation": "The Group feature (layer 15) is dominated by punctuation/markup and tokenization artifacts (e.g. '*', bracket fragments, 'tag') alongside biomedical/web words (cells, ads, incentive), suggesting it responds to emphasis/annotation and fragmented tokens. The Baseline feature (layer 14) shares several content words (rings, cells, lifestyle, ads, -atives) but includes more general technical and notation tokens (plant, space, pass, math symbols like '|$'), indicating a broader topical/technical sensitivity. The Jaccard ~0.29 reflects this partial lexical overlap with different focal patterns.",
  "tags": ["markup", "biomedical", "notation"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect articles; Group feature mixes in a rare content token",
  "explanation": "Both the Group-SAE (layer 15) and Baseline-SAE (layer 14) primarily activate on English articles (\"the\", \"a\", \"an\"). The Baseline feature is a broad, general article detector across many contexts, while the Group feature shows similar article sensitivity but also picks up the content token \"representatives\" in list/nominal-predicate contexts. The Jaccard of 0.391 indicates moderate overlap: grouping produced a slightly more specialized/mixed feature that blends a strong function-word signal with an occasional lexical cue, rather than a pure article-only detector.",
  "tags": ["function-word", "article-detector", "lexical-mixing"]
}

{
  "title": "Punctuation in bibliographic contexts — periods (initials) vs comma separators",
  "explanation": "Group feature 4086 (layer 15) strongly activates on periods and commas found in reference/author-list contexts, so it appears sensitive to periods used in initials or sentence boundaries (e.g., \"D. M.\") as well as the adjacent commas. Baseline feature 561 (layer 14) mainly activates on commas that separate authors or citation items, i.e., list/citation separators. The low Jaccard (0.232) reflects that the group feature is more specialized to dot+comma patterns (initials/abbreviations) while the baseline is a broader comma/list separator.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "citations", "initials"]
}

{
  "title": "Structured closing punctuation vs. general separators",
  "explanation": "Group-SAE activates on closing punctuation patterns that terminate structured literals and arguments (e.g., '},', '\"},', ']', '}', often in JSON, JS, routes and template snippets), indicating boundaries of objects/arrays and quoted fields. Baseline-SAE is a more generic separator detector (',' , '>', \"'),\") that appears across markup, generics/TeX, and function calls—capturing common delimiter usage rather than structure-specific closers.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "code-structure", "json/templating"]
}

{
  "title": "Organization/affiliation detector — group picks subword/multilingual variants, baseline picks full-word institution nouns",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to organization-related tokens (Council, Center, Association, Society), with a moderate overlap (Jaccard 0.321). The Group-SAE feature also fires on subword fragments and multilingual/orthographic variants (e.g., ut, que, ariat; Centre vs Center), indicating sensitivity to morphology and affixes across languages. The Baseline-SAE feature is a cruder detector of full lexical institution nouns (Association, Society, Council, Academy, Committee).",
  "tags": ["organization","morphology","multilingual","entities"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature emphasizes concrete nouns/topics; baseline emphasizes actions/abstract terms",
  "explanation": "Feature 10501 (Group-SAE) activates on concrete, content-bearing nouns and topical concepts (e.g., skeleton, captain, walls, suicide, ethnicity, invention), suggesting a cluster that captures entity/topic semantics. Feature 10935 (Baseline-SAE) favors verbs and abstract/discourse terms (e.g., plans, won, tempted, rumor, moved, advantage, provide), indicating sensitivity to actions, events, and pragmatic/administrative language. The moderate Jaccard (0.437) implies partial overlap in general usage but a clear divergence in semantic focus: Group-SAE → noun/topic coherence; Baseline-SAE → action/abstract/frequency-driven tokens.",
  "tags": ["noun-focused", "action/abstract", "topical-coherence", "frequency-bias"]
}

{
  "title": "Period / sentence-boundary marker around citations and figure/table labels",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the period token used as a sentence terminator, especially when adjacent to bracketed citations, figure/table references, and caption-like fragments. The Group feature (layer 15) examples cluster around formal citation/table/figure punctuation and inline references, while the Baseline feature (layer 14) shows a similar sensitivity but across a slightly broader set of sentence-ending contexts (e.g., follow-up notes and descriptive captions). Together this suggests these units encode punctuation/structural boundary signals rather than semantic content.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "citations", "sentence-boundary", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "Subword/orthographic fragment detector (polym, iem, em)",
  "explanation": "Both features pick up short orthographic/subword fragments that recur inside technical, biomedical and multilingual tokens (e.g., polym‑, ‑iem‑ in Biopsiematerial, ‑em/‑ym in names and morphisms). The Group-SAE (layer 15) is slightly more focused on a tight set of capitalisation-sensitive fragments (e.g., 'OM') while the Baseline-SAE (layer 14) covers a broader set including lowercase variants ('om') and similar morphological variants. The Jaccard 0.596 indicates substantial overlap: these units mainly encode surface morphology/orthography useful for recognizing technical word pieces rather than a single semantic concept.",
  "tags": ["subword-morphology", "orthography", "technical-terms"]
}

{
  "title": "Math-symbol / linear-algebra tokens",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to single-letter mathematical symbols in linear-algebra style LaTeX (e.g., T, P, b, e, tau) and appear in snippets about matrices, block decompositions, and vectors. The Group-SAE feature (layer 15) covers a slightly broader variety of symbol tokens (adds F, r, A) and thus seems to capture more heterogeneous math-notation contexts, while the Baseline feature (layer 14) is very similar but a bit more focused on repeated occurrences of T, b, P, e and block/tilde matrix patterns. The Jaccard score (~0.56) reflects substantial overlap with some complementary specialization between the two.",
  "tags": ["math-notation", "linear-algebra", "single-letter-symbols"]
}

{
  "title": "Left-parenthesis '(' — parenthetical marker",
  "explanation": "Both features reliably detect the left-parenthesis token '(' that introduces parenthetical material (clarifications, asides, examples, hyphenated notations like “un-)”, citations such as “(among others)”, and analytic remarks). They therefore represent a punctuation/syntactic boundary detector rather than a semantic concept. The group feature (layer 15) and the baseline (layer 14) show very similar activation distributions (moderate overlap), suggesting the same parenthetical-detection function appears across adjacent layers.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "parenthetical", "syntax", "token-level"]
}

{
  "title": "LaTeX subscript markers vs formatting/spacing tokens",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature concentrates on underscore-related tokens that mark subscripts in LaTeX math (\"_\", \"_{\", \"_\\\", \"}_{\"), showing up in variable names and indexed quantities. The Baseline feature mixes that subscript opener (\"_{\") with formatting and spacing tokens (\"rm\", \"~\", \"*~\") and punctuation-adjacent patterns like \")_{\", so it captures more of the surrounding formatting/ punctuation contexts rather than the pure subscript boundary. The Jaccard (~0.43) reflects this partial overlap around the \"_{\" token but diverging focus otherwise.",
  "tags": ["LaTeX_subscripts", "formatting_commands", "punctuation", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "Detection of the definite article \"The\" (often before titles/names)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token \"The\", especially when it begins publication names, media titles, organizations, or other proper nouns. The Jaccard overlap is moderate (0.569): the Group feature emphasizes news/media and institutional names (e.g., The Daily Stormer, The Associated Press, The National Archives), while the Baseline also includes some entertainment/tour/movie contexts and multilingual examples (e.g., The Confessions Tour, The Avengers). Overall these look like high-frequency definite-article detectors that are sensitive to title/name contexts rather than encoding a single semantic category.",
  "tags": ["definite-article", "named-entities", "function-word", "news-titles"]
}

{
  "title": "Underscore as LaTeX subscript vs brace‑underscore pattern",
  "explanation": "Both features detect the underscore token used in mathematical LaTeX (subscripts like k_0, n_a, M_n), but they differ in specificity. Group feature 15761 (layer 15) activates on isolated underscore occurrences across a variety of inline/display math snippets, i.e., a general subscript marker. Baseline feature 1142 (layer 14) also picks up underscores but shows additional sensitivity to the sequence `}_` (an underscore immediately following a closing brace), so it captures braced subscripts and punctuation-adjacent underscore patterns as a distinct token context.",
  "tags": ["LaTeX","subscript","punctuation"]
}

{
  "title": "Detects 'type' in figure/table/citation markup",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token \"type\" inside reference/figure/table markup (e.g., ref- **type** =\"fig\", ref-type=\"fig\", supplementary-material). The high Jaccard (0.758) indicates they capture the same phenomenon—tokens used as attribute names labeling embedded citations/figures/tables—though the group feature (layer 15) shows examples with curly-brace attribute syntax and supplementary tags, while the baseline (layer 14) surfaces more plain ref/table/fig variants.",
  "tags": ["markup", "citation", "metadata", "attributes"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature isolates the question delimiter; Baseline mixes delimiter with Cyrillic subwords",
  "explanation": "Feature 11755 (layer 15) is highly selective for the special token \">Q\" — a dataset question delimiter — appearing across diverse snippets and languages, indicating it encodes a structural/formatted marker. Feature 10635 (layer 14) activates on Cyrillic character n‑grams (\"оз\", \"из\", \"з\") as well as \">Q\", so it captures morphological/subword patterns in Russian text plus some delimiter sensitivity. The moderate Jaccard (0.61) comes from their shared attention to the \">Q\" token while the baseline is more linguistically broad and the group feature is more purely structural.",
  "tags": ["delimiter-token", "structural", "Cyrillic-subword", "multilingual"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature focuses on single-letter math symbols; baseline mixes math and domain tokens",
  "explanation": "Both features capture mathematical variables (high Jaccard = 0.78), but the Group-SAE feature (layer 15) is dominated by single-letter, often uppercase tokens used as symbolic names for matrices/sets/operators (A, B, H, R, L, T, e, f). The Baseline-SAE feature (layer 14) overlaps on those core symbols but also includes more domain- or morphology-specific tokens (e.g., multi-letter 'sl', physics/math identifiers like n, g, h) indicating it groups both variable-symbols and longer lexical tokens. In short, the group feature abstracts the pure symbol-role of single-letter math tokens, while the baseline mixes symbol roles with domain-specific/multi-character tokens.",
  "tags": ["math-symbols", "single-letter-variables", "tokenization-mix"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature picks up topical/plural discourse tokens; baseline favors user/interface words",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE (layer 15) concentrates on topical and discourse-level tokens — plural nouns and sentence connectors like “years”, “careers”, “others”, “agenda”, “surprise”, and “And” — suggesting a higher-level cluster of conversational topics and discourse shifts. The Baseline-SAE (layer 14) emphasizes more user/interface and functional items — “part”, “i”, “manage”, “user”, “today”, “errors” — reflecting concrete actions, pronouns, and UI-related language. The moderate Jaccard (0.613) reflects overlap on shared forum/web tokens (e.g., “messages”, “link”, “na”, “.”) while the two features diverge in abstract vs. operational focus.",
  "tags": ["discourse", "plural-nouns", "interface-terms", "layer-difference"]
}

{
  "title": "Single-letter 'V' token (word-initial / subword artifact)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the single-letter token 'V' / 'v' — a tokenizer artifact that often appears as the first subtoken of words (e.g., V OTE, v ain, V eteran, V SR-10). The high Jaccard (0.68) reflects substantial overlap in examples. The baseline also picks up more internal splits and code/foreign-name cases (e.g., v apor, CUSTOM_ V APIS, Von), while the group feature concentrates more on ordinary word-initial occurrences and capitalized initials.",
  "tags": ["character-token", "tokenization", "case-sensitivity"]
}

{
  "title": "Subtoken for the letter 'b' (word-initial; Group captures uppercase too)",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to the single-character subtoken 'b' that appears at the start of many words and in code identifiers (e.g., 'b pawninc'). The Jaccard overlap is high, indicating they encode essentially the same subword. The Group-SAE feature (layer 15) also activates on the uppercase 'B' (sentence-initial or capitalized words), so it appears to distinguish case variants, whereas the Baseline (layer 14) is dominated by the lowercase 'b'.",
  "tags": ["subword","case-sensitivity","code-and-text"]
}

{
  "title": "Shared subword n-grams: clean suffix/morpheme detector (Group) vs. noisier mixed-token detector (Baseline)",
  "explanation": "Both features pick up frequent short character n-grams (e.g. \"pe\", \"ne\", \"ote\", \"TE\") present across languages and contexts (Jaccard 0.654). The Group-SAE (layer 15) concentrates on short vowel/consonant endings and morphological suffixes (\"ude\", \"ume\", \"rate\") that appear as word-final pieces in Romance/Slavic text—a cleaner linguistic suffix signal. The Baseline-SAE (layer 14) also captures those short n-grams but includes longer, more idiosyncratic substrings and code/token fragments (\"inche\", \"ke\", \"ande\"), making it a noisier, mixed-purpose detector.",
  "tags": ["subword-ngrams", "morphology", "noise-code"]
}

{
  "title": "Subordinating-conjunction / complementizer detector (Group narrower, Baseline broader)",
  "explanation": "Both features detect function words that introduce subordinate clauses (e.g., if, because, that). The Group feature is tighter on causal/conditional and complementizer uses (because, if, that) across legal, technical and narrative snippets, while the Baseline covers a broader set of subordinators and discourse markers (if, where, though, when, because), picking up temporal/locative and contrastive contexts as well.",
  "tags": ["subordinating-conjunction","complementizer","function-words","syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Sentence / document boundary detector",
  "explanation": "Both features activate at sentence- or document-ending contexts. The Group feature is dominated by the end-of-text token (<|endoftext|>) appearing after sentences, lists, code and LaTeX fragments, while the Baseline feature responds to sentence-final punctuation, quotes and newlines (e.g., '.', '\"', '\\n'), so they effectively mark boundaries/terminators. The very high Jaccard (0.984) indicates they capture the same role.",
  "tags": ["sentence-boundary", "punctuation", "document-end"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature is a coherent topical/lexical detector; baseline is noisy/fragments",
