  "tags": ["sequence-boundary", "punctuation", "document-terminator"]
}

{
  "title": "Preposition “in” (both capitalizations)",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the token 'in' and its sentence‑initial capitalized form 'In' across a variety of contexts (e.g., 'In terms', 'in midfield', 'in May'), indicating they are surface-level detectors for the high-frequency preposition/case variants. The high Jaccard (0.815) shows they largely duplicate behavior despite coming from adjacent layers, with little apparent semantic specificity beyond recognizing the function word and its capitalization.",
  "tags": ["function-word", "capitalization", "high-frequency"]
}

{
  "title": "Single-letter variable tokens — Group focuses on differentiation, Baseline on function/assignment contexts",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on single-letter variable tokens common in math problems, but they diverge in context. Group-SAE (layer 15) preferentially lights up on variables appearing inside derivative/second-derivative and polynomial expressions (exponentiation, ‘find the derivative’ snippets). Baseline-SAE (layer 14) instead appears in function definitions, assignments, and evaluation contexts (tokens inside f(x)-style or Let statements and function calls). The high Jaccard overlap reflects their shared sensitivity to single-letter variables, while layer/context differences reflect specialization to calculus vs. function/assignment usages.",
  "tags": ["math-variables", "calculus", "function-notation"]
}

{
  "title": "Feature detects citation/reference tokens (fig/table refs)",
  "explanation": "Both Group-SAE (layer 15) and Baseline-SAE (layer 14) strongly activate on the same citation/reference tokens and inline reference markup (e.g., Fig., Table, [ref]-type annotations) across scientific-text snippets. The very high Jaccard (0.928) indicates these features largely overlap in function, reliably flagging citation markers and associated reference patterns rather than semantic content.",
  "tags": ["citation", "reference-marker", "markup"]
}

{
  "title": "Tab character: general indentation vs C-style code idioms",
  "explanation": "Both features center on the tab character as a top token, but they specialize differently. Group-SAE feature 3893 activates on tabs used as indentation and structural separators across multiple languages (Go, Python, config snippets, comment-adjacent lines), capturing generic block/formatting contexts. Baseline-SAE feature 15835 activates on tabs in C-style, low-level code patterns (pattern matching loops, pointer/macro idioms, character-class logic), so it reflects older/lexical C constructs. The Jaccard (0.318) shows moderate overlap (shared whitespace signal) but distinct contextual niches.",
  "tags": ["whitespace", "indentation", "code-structure"]
}

