{
  "title": "Period (sentence-final) vs. newline/HTML paragraph markers",
  "explanation": "The Group feature strongly and narrowly responds to the period token ('.') used to end sentences in running text. The Baseline feature instead fires on structural/formatting tokens — newlines, paragraph breaks and occasional '>' HTML markers — capturing layout or markup boundaries rather than the simple sentence-final punctuation. The low Jaccard score reflects this shift from a punctuation-focused detector to a formatting/markup detector.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "formatting", "sentence-boundary"]
}

{
  "title": "LaTeX/math punctuation and operator markers",
  "explanation": "Both features pick out LaTeX/math formula punctuation and operator-heavy zones. The Group feature is biased toward backslash-led tokens and linebreak/command markers (\\, \\sum, \\frac, double backslashes and double braces), while the Baseline feature highlights relational/operator tokens and closing punctuation (leq, =, +, combinations like )}=\\, ]}). Together they mark formula boundaries and densely symbolic math expressions.",
  "tags": ["LaTeX", "math-punctuation", "operators", "brackets"]
}

{
  "title": "Group feature encodes motion/travel content; baseline encodes pronouns/function words",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature (3509) activates on words tied to movement and travel—way, headed, cab, by, you—so it appears to capture semantic content about direction/transport and short travel contexts (the stray \")\" is likely a tokenizer artifact). The Baseline-SAE feature (3119) instead lights up on sentence-level function items and actors—we, We, they, and, them, 're, 's—reflecting pronouns and coordination/syntax. The moderate overlap (e.g. \"they\") shows shared activation for agent references while the group feature is more content/scene-specific.",
  "tags": ["motion", "pronouns", "travel", "syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Two '?' detectors with different contextual specializations",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to the question-mark token, but they specialize on different QA contexts. Group-SAE 3763 lights up for time/minutes word-problems (\"What is X minutes after/before ... ?\"), while Baseline-SAE 7623 appears in numeric sequence/\"what is next\" problems. The moderate Jaccard (0.364) reflects shared punctuation activation but distinct surrounding semantics, showing the model learns multiple '?' detectors tuned to different task contexts.",
  "tags": ["punctuation","time-questions","sequence-questions"]
}

{
  "title": "Newline token — alphabetical/header vs. generic index breaks",
  "explanation": "Both features peak on the newline token, but they represent different formatting roles. Group feature 8954 is tied to structural breaks that mark alphabetical or bolded headers and double-line separations (e.g., a bold single-letter header or section boundary), whereas Baseline feature 1921 fires on more generic index/list entry breaks and continuations before capitalized entries or comma-delimited items. The low Jaccard (0.216) indicates they both use the same surface token but encode distinct document-layout signals.",
  "tags": ["newline", "index-header", "list-separator", "formatting"]
}

{
  "title": "France vs French — demonym/country lexical cluster",
  "explanation": "The Group-SAE feature activates on both the adjective/demonym 'French' and the proper noun 'France', showing a broader lexical grouping that covers nationality, cuisine, and place-name uses. The Baseline-SAE feature is more narrowly focused on the token 'French' (adjectival/demonym contexts). The low Jaccard (0.267) indicates partial overlap but different selectivity: group regularization appears to merge related word-forms, while the baseline keeps a tighter, token-specific detector.",
  "tags": ["demonym", "country-name", "lexical-variation"]
}

{
  "title": "Feature detects single-letter hex/ID characters in hashes and session tokens",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on single-letter tokens that appear inside hexadecimal-like identifiers, SHA/git references, and session/cookie strings in code and text (e.g., fragments of hashes or session IDs). The token sets largely overlap (c, b, d, e) so they capture the same phenomenon; the Group feature additionally ranks 'a' highly, suggesting a slight difference in which hex letters are emphasized but the overall role is the same: marking short alphanumeric subtokens inside identifiers.",
  "tags": ["hexadecimal", "identifiers", "hashes", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "Detector for copula / 'to be' forms",
  "explanation": "Both features primarily activate on forms of the verb “to be” (is, are, was) and the clitic ’s across varied contexts, indicating a syntactic/function-word detector that marks copulas and linking verbs rather than specific semantic content. The token overlap is moderate (Jaccard 0.48), and the snippets show the feature firing consistently on grammatical linking constructions.",
  "tags": ["copula", "function-words", "syntax", "verb-forms"]
}

{
  "title": "Hyphenation / compound-word marker",
  "explanation": "Both features clearly encode the '-' token as a punctuation/morphology marker used in compound words and adjectival constructions. The moderate Jaccard (0.482) reflects strong overlap: Group-SAE examples lean toward physics/bibliometric contexts (e.g., left-/right-hand polarized, top quark, high-yield), while Baseline-SAE shows a broader spread across technical domains (etching, duty-cycle, finite-dimensional, word-line). Overall the feature functions as an orthographic boundary detector for hyphenated compounds rather than a semantic concept.",
  "tags": ["punctuation", "hyphenation", "morphology", "orthography"]
}

{
  "title": "'ost' as an orthographic/morphological substring token",
  "explanation": "Both features have the same top subtoken \"ost\" and show moderate overlap (Jaccard 0.472). They mostly pick up a surface substring that appears across many words and languages—internal segments and suffixes (e.g., immunostaining, Clostridium, isosteric, post‑boys, gnostique, zaostrz…). This indicates the tokeniser/feature is responding to orthographic/morphological fragments and boundary positions (prefixes/suffixes, hyphenation/line breaks) rather than a single coherent semantic concept. The Group feature examples lean slightly toward biomedical/technical uses, while the Baseline examples include more multilingual inflectional/derivational occurrences.",
  "tags": ["substring-token", "morphology", "multilingual", "orthography"]
}

{
  "title": "Coordinating conjunction and comma (syntax/function words)",
  "explanation": "Both features predominantly activate on the coordinating conjunction \"and\" and the comma, indicating they function as detectors of coordination and clause/list boundaries across diverse domains. The moderate Jaccard (0.414) shows substantial token overlap—both models learn a similar syntactic signal—but differences in snippet distribution suggest they partition contextual uses slightly differently (e.g., punctuation-separated lists vs. direct conjunctions).",
  "tags": ["conjunction", "punctuation", "syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Detects 'M' + short subword sequences (multilingual name fragments)",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on short vowel‑rich character chunks that appear immediately after a capitalized 'M' (e.g., M üser, M ouvance, M ael). The moderate Jaccard overlap shows they capture the same M+subword pattern, but Group-SAE is skewed toward umlauted/Germanic fragments (ü, ä, ö, ael, och) while Baseline-SAE includes more Romance/other fragments (ou, ouss, oc, ith, av). This suggests the feature is picking up a tokenization/orthographic cue—names or morpheme pieces following an isolated 'M'—with Group-SAE being more focused on diacritics and Germanic variants.",
  "tags": ["multilingual", "M-prefix", "diacritics", "subword-ngrams"]
}

{
  "title": "Concrete technical noun cluster (Group) vs measurement/instrumentation tilt (Baseline)",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on mid-frequency, concrete content nouns across domains (sharing tokens like “point”, “velocity”, “certification”), explaining the moderate Jaccard (0.574). The Group feature (7259) emphasizes a heterogeneous set of domain-specific entities (medical, materials, legal — e.g., antibiotics, lattice, products, shielding), whereas the Baseline feature (7975) leans more toward measurement/instrumentation and systems-related terms (distribution, wavelengths, systems, scanner). In short: both capture concrete technical nouns, but Group is broader topical content while Baseline favors measurement/engineering vocabulary.",
  "tags": ["concrete-nouns", "technical-terms", "measurement-vs-topical"]
}

{
  "title": "Both features detect 'www' (URLs); Group leans toward protocol/markup contexts",
  "explanation": "Both features clearly represent the 'www' host token in URLs, as shown by many http/https links in their top snippets. The moderate Jaccard (0.439) indicates partial overlap: Group feature 9863 tends to appear in more technical/embedded contexts (e.g., explicit protocol tokens, headers like \"referer:\", code-like assignments such as \"url =\", login modal paths), while Baseline feature 12798 appears across general prose and conversational link mentions (news, discussion posts). In short, both encode URL host occurrences but with different contextual preferences.",
  "tags": ["urls", "www", "protocol", "markup-context"]
}

{
  "title": "Short subword/suffix detector spanning code, HTML and scientific text",
  "explanation": "Both features respond to frequent short character n-grams or subword suffixes that appear across code, HTML/CSS, email handles and scientific writing (e.g., ec, inc, unc, nanoc, oc). The high Jaccard (0.607) reflects this overlap. The Baseline feature leans more on the adjectival/technical suffix 'ic' (e.g., '...ic', 'multicasting'), while the Group feature highlights uppercase or identifier fragments like 'SC' and 'uc' (e.g., CSS classes, C struct fields). Overall they capture form/word-piece patterns rather than coherent high-level semantics.",
  "tags": ["subword","suffix","code/html","scientific"]
}

{
  "title": "Detection of the token \"in\" (case-insensitive)",
  "explanation": "Both features predominantly activate on the same short function word tokens \"in\" / \"In\" used as a preposition or discourse marker (sentence-initial, connective phrases, and occurrences broken by line breaks or hyphenation). The high overlap (Jaccard 0.608) indicates they are largely redundant. The baseline variant covers a slightly wider set of connective uses (e.g., \"in any event\", \"in turn\"), while the group variant appears a bit more concentrated on sentence‑initial or emphasized instances, but the difference is minor.",
  "tags": ["stopword", "preposition", "case-insensitive", "sentence-initial"]
}

{
  "title": "Colon (:) — shared token, Group slightly skewed to technical/structured contexts",
  "explanation": "Both features have ':' as the top token and show high overlap (Jaccard 0.742): they capture colons in code/CLI flags, key:value pairs, time/ratio-like fragments, and prose punctuation. The Group-SAE snippets emphasize programmatic/formatting uses (CSS pseudo-selectors, command flags, native-image/picocli examples, numeric patterns), whereas the Baseline also includes similar examples but leans a bit more toward general/multilingual prose instances. Overall the difference is subtle: Group is slightly more specialized to structured/technical colon usage.",
  "tags": ["colon", "punctuation", "code-syntax"]
}

{
  "title": "Curly apostrophe (’): possessive vs. contraction contexts",
  "explanation": "Both features strongly activate on the curly apostrophe token (Jaccard 0.759), but their contexts differ: Group feature 9079 appears more in formal/news possessive uses with named entities (e.g., Scotland ’ s, John ’ s) and editorial text, while Baseline feature 7992 surfaces more in quoted, conversational or sports-reporting contractions (e.g., It ’ s, he ’ s). Both reflect a tokenization pattern that isolates the apostrophe as its own token.",
  "tags": ["apostrophe", "possessive", "contraction", "tokenization"]
}

{
  "title": "Entities and legal/technical mix vs diffuse technical/legal feature",
  "explanation": "The Group feature centers on concrete nouns and entity/record contexts (e.g., overturned, files, Name, criminals, mice, gravity) suggesting a detector for people/records/subject-matter mentions. The Baseline feature is more diffuse, picking up technical or legal/structural tokens (voltage, 501, substitutions, workload) alongside shared surface fragments/abbreviations (ors, Ps, pole), which explains the moderate Jaccard overlap—common suffixes and abbreviations rather than identical semantics.",
  "tags": ["entities", "legal", "technical", "fragments"]
}

{
  "title": "Opening square bracket '[' — Group vs Baseline",
  "explanation": "Both features activate on the '[' token (Jaccard 0.665), reflecting general detection of bracketed spans, but with different emphases. Group-SAE 913 highlights inline/bracketing uses in prose and logs (links, names, timestamps, checksums, vendor codes, brief code fragments), while Baseline-SAE 13048 leans more toward structured/markup and technical contexts (regex/LaTeX/Markdown notation, footnotes, shell conditionals and table annotations). In short, both capture '[' as a delimiting/punctuation signal, but Group-SAE is more conversational/log-focused and Baseline-SAE more markup/math/code-focused.",
